body.translated_text:
) repeating those very old words and syllables, say thus: 'Alas! Alas!' Just so, those beings, when the earth-crust disappeared, coming together and assembling, grieved, lamented, and wailed. They said thus: 'Alas! Alas!' But they do not know the meaning: 'This is the meaning of that saying, this is the meaning of that saying.'
When the earth-crust disappeared, for those beings the forest creeper (<s>vanalatā</s>) appeared, endowed with color, endowed with fragrance, endowed with flavor. Such was its color — like a <s>kadambaka</s> flower. Such was its flavor — like pure, unadulterated bee-honey. They ate that as their food and sustenance, long-lived, abiding for a long duration. Among them, whoever consumed less food became beautiful in complexion; whoever consumed more food became ugly in complexion. Thus, depending on the twofold distinction in food, a twofold distinction in complexion became apparent. When the twofold distinction in complexion existed, one being despised another: 'Ha! Being, I am beautiful in complexion; you are ugly!' Because of those beings who were proud of their complexion, because of their adoption of those very sinful, unwholesome qualities, the forest creeper disappeared.
When the forest creeper disappeared, those beings, coming together and assembling, grieved, lamented, and wailed. They said thus: 'Go away! Go away!' Just so, those beings, when the forest creeper disappeared, coming together and assembling, grieved, lamented, and wailed. They said: 'Go away! Go away!' But they do not know the meaning: 'This is the meaning of that saying, this is the meaning of that saying.' When the forest creeper disappeared, O <s>Gautamas</s>, for those beings there appeared self-sown rice bearing grain-fruit, without husk, without chaff, pure, clean, four finger-breadths tall, growing in clusters. When cut in the evening, it ripened by morning (

