children.translated_text:
) remained in his mother's womb, at that time the mother for her entire life undertook the five precepts (<s>pañcavratapadāni</s>): for her entire life she abandoned the taking of life and refrained from taking life; she abandoned taking what is not given, unchaste conduct, false speech, and the ground of heedlessness through wine, spirits, and intoxicants, and refrained from the ground of heedlessness through wine, spirits, and intoxicants.
She did not kill living beings, did not take what was not given; she did not speak falsely; she was not addicted to intoxicants;
she refrained from unchaste conduct and sexual intercourse — the mother of <s>Siddhārtha</s>; this is the natural law here.
It is the natural law that at the time when the <s>Bodhisattva</s> <s>Bhagavān</s> remained in his mother's womb, the mother at that time did not fix her mind upon men with thoughts connected with sensual desire.
She is not defiled by the afflictions (<s>kleśa</s>); nor does her mind burn on account of desire;
nor does his mother fix her mind upon men with thoughts connected with the strands of sensual pleasure.

