What do you think, monks? He who was the caravan leader <s>Viśvabhuj</s> — that was none other than myself at that time, in that era. The five merchants who became extremely agitated from lack of water — they are these very five monks. Even then I satisfied them with water and brought them across the wilderness road. And now too I have satisfied them with the nectar of the true <s>Dharma</s> and brought them across the wilderness of <s>saṃsāra</s>.
When the <s>Bhagavān</s> had brought the group of five monks across the wilderness of <s>saṃsāra</s> and shared with them the jewels of the faculties, powers, and factors of awakening, the monks, having doubts arise, asked the <s>Buddha</s> <s>Bhagavān</s>, who cuts through all doubts: 'See, Venerable Sir, the <s>Bhagavān</s> has brought the group of five monks across the wilderness of <s>saṃsāra</s> and shared with them the jewels of the faculties, powers, and factors of awakening.' The <s>Bhagavān</s> said: 'What is wonderful about this, monks, that now I, who am free from passion, free from hatred, free from delusion, liberated from birth, old age, disease, death, sorrow, lamentation, suffering, dejection, and despair, omniscient, knowing all aspects, having attained mastery over all knowledge and knowable things, have brought the group of five monks across the wilderness of <s>saṃsāra</s> and shared with them the jewels of the faculties, powers, and factors of awakening

