) in various arts and crafts. He had tied precious jewels at his waist. The vessel was wrecked. They clung to the caravan leader. With his exhausted body, barely alive, he brought them to the ocean shore. When they reached dry land, they released him. Utterly spent, he died. They had been brought from water to dry land by him. Then they saw the precious jewels tied at his waist. Taking those, they departed.
What do you think, monks? He who was the caravan leader named <s>Dhanaratha</s> — that was none other than myself at that time, in that era. The five monks — even then I brought them across the great ocean and shared jewels with them. And now too I have brought them across the wilderness of <s>saṃsāra</s> and shared with them the jewels of the faculties, powers, and factors of awakening.
When the Venerable <s>Ājñātakauṇḍinya</s> sat at the head of the assembly, he sat shading the assembly like an umbrella. The monks, having doubts arise, asked the <s>Buddha</s> <s>Bhagavān</s>, who cuts through all doubts: 'What deed, Venerable Sir, was done by the Venerable <s>Ājñātakauṇḍinya</s>, by the ripening of which deed he sits shading the assembly like an umbrella?' The <s>Bhagavān</s> said: 'The monk <s>Kauṇḍinya</s> performed deeds, accumulated them, gathered their provisions — as before, down to: deeds indeed bear fruit for embodied beings.'

