body.translated_text:
) 'Sirs, why should we fear? Our king is one whose nature is loving-kindness, who is compassionate, who three times a day enters the meditation of loving-kindness with a mind intent on the welfare of all beings.' Then those <s>yakṣas</s>, having transformed themselves into the guise of <s>Brāhmaṇas</s>, inspected the alms-halls at the four gates of <s>Vārāṇasī</s>, and knowing that King <s>Vajrabāhu</s> had emerged from the meditation of loving-kindness, approached the king in humble attire. Having approached, they congratulated the king with wishes of victory and long life and said: 'Your Majesty, we are hungry. Show favor with food.' The king summoned his ministers and said: 'Sirs, satisfy these <s>Brāhmaṇas</s> with excellent food.' They said: 'We eat flesh and blood.' The king instructed his ministers: 'Sirs, if there is no supply of meat or blood, give them blood obtained in a lawful and righteous manner from the marketplace.' They said: 'We eat the flesh and blood of freshly slaughtered [animals]. Show favor with freshly slaughtered flesh and blood.' The king reflected: 'Where is a supply of freshly slaughtered flesh and blood to be had without causing harm to another? Let me give them my own blood.' Then, having summoned the physicians, he said: 'Sirs, make incisions in all my veins throughout my body. I shall satisfy the beggars.' The physicians said: 'We shall not lay a blade upon Your Majesty's body for the sake of common men.' <s>Bodhisattvas</s> are skilled in various arts and crafts. He himself made incisions in five limbs. Then, having satisfied them with his own blood, he gave them such a teaching on the <s>Dharma</s> that, having heard it, those <s>yakṣas</s> were established in the five precepts.
  What do you think, monks? He who was the king named <s>Vajrabāhu</s> — that was I at that time, in that era. Those five essence-stealing <s>yakṣas</s> — they are these five monks. At that time too, having satisfied them with my own blood, I established them in the five precepts. Now too, having satisfied them with the nectar of the true <s>Dharma</s>, I have brought them across the wilderness of <s>saṃsāra</s> and established them in the ultimate security of <s>nirvāṇa</s>.
  When the <s>Bhagavān</s> had satisfied the group of five monks with the nectar of the true <s>Dharma</s>, brought them across the wilderness of <s>saṃsāra</s>, and established them in the ultimate security of <s>nirvāṇa</s>, then the monks, doubts having arisen, asked the <s>Buddha</s> <s>Bhagavān</s>, who cuts through all doubts: 'See, Venerable Sir — the <s>Bhagavān</s> has satisfied the group of five monks with the nectar of the true <s>Dharma</s>, (

body.text:
) ājñā dattā: bhavanta adhiṣṭhāne evaṃvidhaṃ ghaṇṭāvaghoṣaṇaṃ kārayata; yāvanto puruṣāḥ prativasanti taiḥ sarvair niṣparihārair bhūtvā maṇḍalavāṭaṃ praveṣṭavyaṃ; yo na praviśati tasya vadho daṇḍa iti; āmatyair ghaṇṭāvaghoṣaṇā kāritā; sarve 'dhiṣṭhānanivāsinaḥ puruṣā praviṣṭāḥ; tato rājñā tasmai dārakāya mālāṃ datvā uktaṃ: yas tava pitā tasmai tāṃ mālām anuprayaccha iti; ārakṣakāś ca puruṣā uktāḥ: yasyāyaṃ dārako mālāṃ dadāti yuṣmābhir gṛhītvā matsakāśam upaneyaḥ iti; tatas tāṃ puṣpamālāṃ gṛhītvā paribhramitum ārabdho janakāyān nirīkṣamāṇaḥ; tenāsau coro dṛṣṭaḥ; acintyaḥ satvānāṃ karmavipākaḥ; tena tasmai mālā dattā; rājapuruṣai ekacoro gṛhītvā rājña upanāmitaḥ; rājā amātyān āmantrayate: bhavantaḥ katham atra pratipattavyaṃ? iti; te kathayanti; deva praghātyatām iti;
rājā kathayati: bhavantaḥ katham īdṛśo vīrapuruṣaḥ praghātyaḥ; upasaṃgraho 'sya kartavyaḥ iti; tena tasmai sā duhitā sarvālaṃkāravibhūṣitā bhāryārthaṃ dattā; upārdharājyena saṃvibhaktaḥ.
  kiṃ manyadhve bhikṣavaḥ? yo 'sāv ekacorika aham eva sa tena kālena tena samayena; yo 'sau tasya putraḥ eṣa evāsau rāhulas tena kālena tena samayena; tadāpy anena mālayā vijñātaḥ; etarhy apy aham anena modakena vijñātaḥ; evaṃ hi vo bhikṣavo 'cintyaḥ satvānāṃ karmavipākaḥ iti karmaparāyaṇair bhavitavyaṃ.

body.translated_text:
) 'Sirs, have a bell-proclamation made at the capital as follows: All men who dwell there must come, without exception, stripped of all coverings, into the arena. Whoever does not enter shall receive the death penalty.' The ministers had the bell-proclamation made. All men dwelling in the capital entered. Then the king, having given the boy a garland, said: 'Give this garland to your father.' And the guards were told: 'To whomever this boy gives the garland, you must seize him and bring him to me.' Then, having taken the flower-garland, the boy began to wander about, looking at the crowd of people. He spotted that thief. Inconceivable is the ripening of <s>karma</s> (deeds) for beings. He gave the garland to him. The royal officers seized the lone thief and presented him to the king. The king addressed his ministers: 'Sirs, what should be done here?' They said: 'Your Majesty, let him be executed.'
The king said: 'Sirs, how can such a heroic man be executed? He should be taken into our service.' He gave him that daughter, adorned with all ornaments, as a wife. He shared half the kingdom with him.
  What do you think, monks? He who was the lone thief — that was none other than myself at that time, in that era. He who was his son — that is none other than <s>Rāhula</s> at that time, in that era. Even then I was identified by him through a garland. Now too I have been identified by him through a sweet-ball. Thus indeed, monks, inconceivable is the ripening of <s>karma</s> for beings — one should be devoted to <s>karma</s> and its consequences.

