) by friendly counsel. But though admonished by friendly counsel, they persist in holding firmly to that same matter and insist: "This alone is the truth; all else is delusion."' The <s>Bhagavān</s> said: 'Admonish <s>Kokālika</s>, <s>Khaṇḍadravya</s>, <s>Kaṭamoraka</s>, <s>Tiṣya</s>, and <s>Samudradatta</s>, monks, by means of the formal act of the fourth motion (<s>jñapticaturtha karma</s>).' And any others of the same kind should likewise be admonished.
  Having arranged seats, having struck the gong, having summoned the monks by personal notification, when the entire <s>Saṅgha</s> is assembled and gathered, one monk should make the motion (<s>jñapti</s>) and the formal act is to be performed:
  'Let the venerable <s>Saṅgha</s> hear! These — <s>Kokālika</s>, <s>Khaṇḍadravya</s>, <s>Kaṭamoraka</s>, <s>Tiṣya</s>, and <s>Samudradatta</s> — knowing that a monk is striving for the schism of the united <s>Saṅgha</s>, are acting as his followers in the disruptive speech. And they say to the monks: "Do not, venerable ones, say anything to this monk — whether good or bad. And why? This monk, venerable ones, is one who speaks the <s>Dharma</s>, one who speaks the <s>Vinaya</s>. This monk upholds and maintains the <s>Dharma</s> and the <s>Vinaya</s>. This monk speaks with knowledge, not without knowledge. What is agreeable and acceptable to this monk is agreeable and acceptable to us as well." Though admonished by many monks through friendly counsel, they persist in holding firmly to that same matter and insist: "This alone is the truth; all else is delusion." If the <s>Saṅgha</s> deems the time appropriate, may the <s>Saṅgha</s> consent that the <s>Saṅgha</s> admonish <s>Kokālika</s>, <s>Khaṇḍadravya</s>, <s>Kaṭamoraka</s>, <s>Tiṣya</s>, and <s>Samudradatta</s> by means of the formal act of the fourth motion: "Do not, <s>Kokālika</s>, <s>Khaṇḍadravya</s>, <s>Kaṭamoraka</s>, <s>Tiṣya</s>, and <s>Samudradatta</s>, knowing that a monk is striving for the schism of the united <s>Saṅgha</s> and who, having taken up and maintained a dispute conducive to schism, stands [in it] — be followers of his in the disruptive speech. And do not say to the monks: 'Do not, venerable ones, say anything to this monk — whether good or bad. And why? This monk is one who speaks the <s>Dharma</s>, one who speaks the <s>Vinaya</s>. This monk upholds and maintains the <s>Dharma</s> and the <s>Vinaya</s>. This monk speaks with knowledge, not without knowledge. What is agreeable and acceptable to this monk is agreeable and acceptable to us as well.' And why? This monk is not one who speaks the <s>Dharma</s>, nor one who speaks the <s>Vinaya</s>. This monk upholds and maintains what is contrary