Then that divine son, formerly a <s>yakṣa</s>, like a merchant who has gained profit, like a farmer whose harvest is ripe, like a hero who has won the battle, like a sick man freed from all disease — with the same splendor with which he had approached the <s>Bhagavān</s>'s presence, with that same splendor he returned to his own abode.
The monks, who practiced wakefulness in the first and last watches of the night, saw an extraordinary radiance in the <s>Bhagavān</s>'s presence. Having seen it, they asked the <s>Bhagavān</s> in doubt: '<s>Bhagavān</s>, did <s>Brahmā Sahāṃpati</s>, <s>Śakra</s> Lord of the Gods, or the Four World-Guardians come to see the <s>Bhagavān</s> this night?' The <s>Bhagavān</s> said: 'No, monks, neither <s>Brahmā Sahāṃpati</s>, nor <s>Śakra</s> Lord of the Gods, nor the Four World-Guardians came to see me. Rather, <s>Devadatta</s>, striving to kill me, hurled a boulder from <s>Gṛdhrakūṭa</s> mountain with a catapult. It was pulverized in mid-air by <s>Vajrapāṇi</s> with his thunderbolt. Half of it, about to fall upon me, was poorly caught by the <s>yakṣa</s> <s>Kumbhīra</s>. He was killed by it. Having generated faith in my presence, he died and was reborn among the noble <s>Trāyastriṃśa</s> gods

