) A woman — here a maiden is intended. Whatever milk is hers is called 'maiden's milk.' The Venerable <s>Ānanda</s> mobilized all four assemblies for the sake of the maiden's milk. The monks began to search for maiden's milk. The nuns, laymen, and laywomen — indeed nearly all the inhabitants of <s>Rājagṛha</s> — except <s>Devadatta</s> and his partisans. Those [partisans] created obstacles by various means for the four assemblies and the crowd of people who were searching. Then in <s>Rājagṛha</s>, a certain maiden who was a first-time mother, with little milk, could not even satisfy her own child with milk — how much less could she give to another? She heard that the <s>Bhagavān</s> needed maiden's milk. She reflected: 'If I give, my child, who is of feeble vitality, will die from the deprivation of milk. Nor is that the only danger. There is another: <s>Devadatta</s> has long been the <s>Bhagavān</s>'s adversary and enemy. And King <s>Ajātaśatru</s> has been turned against him, as have <s>Devadatta</s>'s partisans — <s>Kokālika</s>, <s>Khaṇḍadravya</s>, <s>Kaṭamoraka Tiṣya</s>, and <s>Samudradatta</s>. They will take my life.' Then she reflected again: 'Better the loss of my own life and the loss of my son than that the <s>Buddha</s> <s>Bhagavān</s>, worshipped by gods and humans, should endure the suffering of sickness, on account of which all the inhabitants of <s>Rājagṛha</s> are in turmoil. Let me take the milk and go.' Having placed her faith in the <s>Bhagavān</s>, she milked her milk into a new vessel and approached the <s>Bhagavān</s>. Having approached, she bowed her head at the <s>Bhagavān</s>'s feet and said to the <s>Bhagavān</s>: 'I have obtained, Venerable Sir, the maiden's milk that <s>Jīvaka</s> prescribed for the <s>Bhagavān</s>. May this maiden's milk be accepted, for my benefit.' There the <s>Bhagavān</s> addressed the Venerable <s>Ānanda</s>: 'This girl is of good intentions, <s>Ānanda</s>. Accept the milk from her.' He accepted the milk. She bowed her head at the <s>Bhagavān</s>'s feet and departed from the <s>Bhagavān</s>'s presence.
  Then the <s>Bhagavān</s> made a display of wonder. For it is the nature of things that at the time when <s>Buddhas</s> and <s>Bhagavāns</s> manifest a smile, rays of blue, yellow, red, and white issue from their mouths. Some go downward; some go upward.
  Those that go downward go to the hells — <s>Sañjīva</s>, <s>Kālasūtra</s>, <s>Saṅghāta</s>, <s>Raurava</s>, <s>Mahāraurava</s>, <s>Tāpana</s>, <s>Pratāpana</s>, <s>Avīci</s>, <s>Arbuda</s>, <s>Nirarbuda</s>, <s>Aṭaṭa</s>, <s>Hahava</s>, <s>Huhuva</s>, <s>Utpala</s>, <s>Padma</s>, and <s>Mahāpadma</s>. In the hot hells, they fall as cold; in the cold hells, they fall as hot. By that, the particular sufferings of those beings are relieved. They think: 'Have we, sirs, departed from here, or have we been reborn elsewhere?' To generate faith in them, the <s>Bhagavān</s> sends forth an emanation. Having seen the emanation, they think: 'We have not, sirs, departed from here, nor have we been reborn elsewhere. Rather, this is a being of unprecedented appearance. By his power, our particular sufferings have been relieved.' Having placed their faith in the emanation, having exhausted the <s>karma</s> to be experienced in the hells, they take rebirth among gods and humans, where they become fit vessels for the truths.

