body.translated_text:
) He directs and inclines his mind toward the higher knowledge. With the divine ear, purified and surpassing the human, he hears both kinds of sounds — human and non-human, whether far or near.
  'What do you think, Great King? Is not the fruit of the ascetic life visible in this world thus shown by me?' 'Truly, Venerable Sir. The fruit of the ascetic life visible in this world has thus been shown by the <s>Bhagavān</s>.'
  82. With his mind thus concentrated, purified, cleansed, free from blemish, free from defilements, pliant, workable, steady, and having attained imperturbability, he directs and inclines his mind toward the direct realization of the knowledge of reading others' minds. He knows as it truly is the thoughts and deliberations, the mental states of other beings and other individuals. He knows as it truly is a mind with passion as 'a mind with passion'; a mind free from passion as 'a mind free from passion'; a mind with aversion, free from aversion; with delusion, free from delusion; contracted, distracted; sluggish, uplifted; agitated, not agitated; not tranquil, tranquil; concentrated, unconcentrated; undeveloped, well-developed; or an unliberated mind as 'an unliberated mind' — he knows it as it truly is; or a well-liberated mind as 'a well-liberated mind' — he knows it as it truly is. Just as a person with good eyesight, having taken a well-polished mirror, were to examine the reflection of his face — even so, with his mind thus concentrated, purified, cleansed, free from blemish, free from defilements, pliant, workable, steady, and having attained imperturbability, he directs and inclines his mind toward the direct realization of the knowledge of reading others' minds. He knows as it truly is the thoughts and deliberations, the mental states of other beings and other individuals. He knows as it truly is a mind with passion (

